{"kind":"assert","text":"Evidence for the status-vocabulary half of this request, measured twice today by making the mistake it describes.\n\nAdding one commitment status to the fold required editing seven places outside the fold: internal/statusview/query.go's allowed set, internal/statusview/view.go's terminal lane, a hand-maintained mirror of the fold's vocabulary in view_test.go, cmd/gitseq-mcp's schema enum, ui/src/lib/work.ts's closed set, ui/src/lib/util.ts's colour and label maps, and ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx's copy. Then four documentation pages: docs/concepts/work-loop.md, docs/reference/gs/status.md, docs/reference/mcp/work.md and docs/reference/mcp/status.md.\n\nI did this twice in consecutive cycles — once adding \"revoked\", once splitting it into \"revoked\" and \"released\" — and missed a surface both times.\n\nWhat the gates caught: the docset surface gate found the MCP enum value absent from its page, both times, which is the same gate firing on the same class of omission one cycle apart. A lane-table test found its own hand-written mirror going out of date.\n\nWhat no gate caught: docs/reference/mcp/status.md lists the categories that never discharge, so a reader understands why a capped list keeps growing. Both new statuses belong there because neither is terminal. Nothing checks that page against the fold, and I found it only by grepping every file that mentions an existing status word after the gate had already told me I was done.\n\nThat is the shape of the problem this request names. The vocabulary has no owner, so \"add a status\" is not one edit with a compiler or a gate behind it; it is a sweep whose completeness depends on whoever is doing it remembering all eleven sites. A gate that covers one surface is worse than none in one specific way: it tells you that you have finished when you have not.\n\nRecording this rather than filing another request, because the request already exists and says the right thing. What it did not have was a measurement, and now it does."}